---
import ProblemReportTable from "../../components/ProblemReportTable.astro";
import Document from "../../layouts/Document.astro";
import { contact, controller } from "../../data/privacy-contact.mjs";

const core = "https://github.com/frostyard/core/blob/main";
const intake = "https://github.com/frostyard/problem-intake/blob/main";
// ADR-0074 at the commit that merged it, so this page cites the text it states.
const adr0074 = "https://github.com/frostyard/core/blob/ed1fcbde60c63fcacd39d0e0db725f0b40e56a88/docs/adr/0074-publish-verified-fault-records-as-public-issues.md";
const issueSearch = "https://github.com/search?q=org%3Afrostyard+label%3Aproblem-report&type=issues";
// The publication cutoff. The triage job of frostyard/problem-intake reads this
// date from the live page and files only faults first seen on or after 00:00 UTC
// of it (ADR-0074). Change it only together with that job's configuration.
const cutoffIso = "2026-11-20";
const cutoffText = new Date(`${cutoffIso}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString("en-GB", { day: "numeric", month: "long", year: "numeric", timeZone: "UTC" });
const publicFields: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ["app_problem", "<code>app_id</code>, <code>runtime</code>, <code>component</code>, and <code>code</code> (class <code>error</code>) or <code>frames</code> or <code>location</code> (class <code>panic</code>)"],
  ["unit_failed", "<code>unit</code>, <code>result</code>"],
  ["update_stage_failed", "<code>error_class</code>, <code>target_version</code>"],
  ["booted_older_image", "<code>how</code>, <code>newer_version</code>"],
  ["sysext_update_failed", "<code>feature</code>, <code>outcome</code>, <code>error_class</code> if the report has it, and the newest checked extension <code>version</code> if there is one"],
  ["tpm_unlock", "<code>outcome</code>, <code>tpm_rc</code>, the variant"],
  ["integrity_error", "<code>subsystem</code>, the variant"],
];
const sources: Array<[string, string, string]> = [
  ["The decision", `${core}/docs/adr/0069-report-problems-local-first-opt-in-with-one-sender-per-host.md`, "frostyard/core ADR-0069: local first, opt-in, one sender per computer."],
  ["Public bug reports", adr0074, "frostyard/core ADR-0074, at the commit that accepted it: which values of a fault may appear in a public issue."],
  ["The triage job", `${intake}/docs/specs/triage.md`, "frostyard/problem-intake: how the job checks each value, files an issue, and follows the fix."],
  ["Every public bug report", issueSearch, "GitHub search: org:frostyard label:problem-report."],
  ["The report format", `${core}/docs/specs/problem-report.md`, "frostyard/core: every field, every value, and the rules for new versions."],
  ["The app library", "https://github.com/frostyard/std/tree/main/problems", "frostyard/std: the Go package that writes app problems to the local journal. The Rust crate is in rust/."],
  ["The collector", "https://github.com/frostyard/snosi", "frostyard/snosi: snosi-health, which lists system problems and sends them on Floe."],
  ["The Floe sender", "https://github.com/frostyard/snosi/blob/main/docs/adr/0030-floe-sends-problem-reports-with-admin-consent.md", "frostyard/snosi ADR-0030: how Floe sends reports after an administrator agrees."],
  ["ChairLift", "https://github.com/frostyard/chairlift/blob/main/docs/adr/0015-problems-page-and-opt-in-reporting.md", "frostyard/chairlift: the Problems page and the desktop sender."],
  ["The intake", "https://github.com/frostyard/problem-intake", "frostyard/problem-intake: the service at report.frostyard.org that checks reports and drops your IP address."],
  ["The statistics", `${intake}/docs/specs/stats.md`, "frostyard/problem-intake: how the weekly statistics are counted and which numbers are hidden."],
  ["Retention and deletion", `${intake}/docs/runbook/glitchtip.md`, "frostyard/problem-intake: the retention limit, the snapshots, and how 1 report is deleted."],
];
---
<Document title="Problem reports | Frostyard" description="What Frostyard problem reports send, what they never send, how long they are kept, and your rights." eyebrow="Privacy" heading="Problem reports">
  <p class="lede">Frostyard images and apps can send short problem reports, so we can find and fix faults. Reporting is off until you turn it on. This page says what a report holds, where it goes, how long we keep it, and how to have it deleted.</p>
  <p><strong>Status:</strong> ChairLift v0.15.0 and later sends reports on Snow, Snowfield and Sundog after you turn reporting on. Those images get it with the next image release. On Floe, snosi-health sends reports after an administrator turns reporting on.</p>

  <h2 id="what-reports-are-for">What reports are for</h2>
  <p>Reports tell us which updates fail, which services break, which Frostyard apps hit errors, and which versions are in use. Each kind of report and its purpose is in <a href="#what-is-sent">What is sent</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="turn-it-on-or-off">Turn reporting on or off</h2>
  <p>Nothing is sent until you turn reporting on. When you turn it off, sending stops at once. The controls are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Snow, Snowfield and Sundog:</strong> ChairLift's Problems page has a <em>Send problem reports</em> switch. On Snow and Snowfield, ChairLift's first-run setup can also ask you, with the switch off. Each user of the computer decides for themselves. In <em>Ask each time</em>, ChairLift shows you the exact report before it sends it. In <em>Send automatically</em>, it sends without asking, and its <em>Sent reports</em> list shows the 50 newest reports it sent.</li>
    <li><strong>Floe:</strong> an administrator runs <code>sudo snosi-health reporting on</code> or <code>off</code>. <code>snosi-health reporting status</code> shows the choice. <code>snosi-health send --dry-run</code> prints the exact reports without sending them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Frostyard apps never send anything themselves. They write problems to the journal on your computer. ChairLift on a desktop, or snosi-health on Floe, is the only program that sends. A computer that does not run a Frostyard image sends nothing.</p>
  <p>If a new version of the report format adds a kind of report or a field, sending stops until you agree again.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is-sent">What is sent</h2>
  <p>Every report has the fields below, and then the fields of its kind. Every value is a fixed choice, a number in a range, a version, or a name from a fixed list. No field holds free text. The machine-readable format is <a href="/schemas/problem-report/v1.json"><code>/schemas/problem-report/v1.json</code></a>.</p>
  <ProblemReportTable />

  <h2 id="what-is-never-sent">What is never sent</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No machine ID, boot ID, account, user name, host name or cookie.</li>
    <li>No error message, panic message, log text, command line or environment.</li>
    <li>No path, name or content of your files, and no core dump. When crash reports start, a crash report will name only the program that crashed and its package. A Rust app problem names a source file of Frostyard's own code.</li>
    <li>No date or time finer than a week. The intake and GlitchTip record the time a report arrives.</li>
    <li>Nothing about services or extensions that you added yourself.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="your-ip-address">Your IP address</h2>
  <p>Your IP address is personal data. It reaches Cloudflare, which carries the report to <code>report.frostyard.org</code> for us as our processor, under its data processing agreement. The intake uses the address only to limit each address to 10 requests a minute, with a short-lived counter. It does not store, log or forward the address, and it stores no key made from it. The server that keeps reports never receives it: the proxy in front of that server removes the forwarding headers and keeps no log.</p>
  <p>Cloudflare's own security features can record the address of a request that they block or challenge, in Cloudflare's security events.</p>

  <h2 id="where-reports-go">Where reports go and how long we keep them</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reports:</strong> the intake checks each report against the format, then forwards each problem report to GlitchTip, an error tracker on a server in the United States. The weekly count is not forwarded. GlitchTip keeps reports for 72 days. Its nightly cleanup and 7 days of backup snapshots can keep parts of a report up to about 88 days in all. Every report is deleted within 90 days. GlitchTip keeps each value it has seen in a report field, such as an image version or a program name, with no link to any report and with no end date.</li>
    <li><strong>Counts for the statistics:</strong> for each accepted report, the intake also writes 1 data point to Cloudflare Workers Analytics Engine. A data point holds only values from the report, such as the kind, the image and its version. It holds no <code>report_id</code> and no IP address. Cloudflare records the time it writes each point and keeps the points for about 3 months.</li>
    <li><strong>Weekly statistics:</strong> totals for each week, kept in Cloudflare Workers KV with no end date, and published.</li>
    <li><strong>Public bug reports:</strong> GitHub issues about faults in Frostyard code, kept with no end date. They hold only checked values, no report. See <a href="#public-bug-reports">Public bug reports</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="public-statistics">Public statistics</h2>
  <p>We publish weekly totals on the <a href="/stats/">statistics page</a>. A number from 1 to 4 is never published, and neither is a number that would let you work one out from the totals. We never publish single reports. The 1-to-4 rule covers the statistics: a <a href="#public-bug-reports">public bug report</a> holds no count, but it exists only because 1 or more reports came in.</p>

  <h2 id="public-bug-reports">Public bug reports</h2>
  <p>When reports show a fault in Frostyard code, a job can open a public GitHub issue for it, with the label <code>problem-report</code>, in the repository of the app or image that has the fault. Anyone can read these issues: <a href={issueSearch}>list every public bug report</a>. The job runs at most once a day and opens at most 1 issue for each problem. <code>crash</code> and <code>count</code> reports never open an issue.</p>
  <p>Before the job publishes a value, it finds that value in a public source of Frostyard: a release tag, the source code at that tag, a published image tag, the published extension index, or the job's fixed list of Frostyard services and extensions. A value that fails its check is never published.</p>

  <h3 id="public-bug-reports-holds">What an issue holds</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The kind and the class of the problem.</li>
    <li>The newest version that the job checked: the app version for an app problem, else each image with its newest image version. Never a list of versions.</li>
    <li>The fields of its kind in the table below, and no other field.</li>
    <li>A problem key: a short hash of the published values only.</li>
    <li>The numbers of the matching issues in our GlitchTip. These are GlitchTip's own issue numbers, not report IDs.</li>
    <li>Later comments from the job hold only fixed text, a marker, and the fix releases and versions that it checked.</li>
  </ul>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Kind</th><th scope="col">Fields in the issue</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{publicFields.map(([kind, fields]) => <tr><td><code>{kind}</code></td><td set:html={fields} /></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table>
  <p>A Go frame of another module shows only its top package name. The variant tells if the image is the standard or the secure-boot image (the report's <code>secure</code>). Only <code>tpm_unlock</code> and <code>integrity_error</code> show it.</p>

  <h3 id="public-bug-reports-never">What an issue never holds</h3>
  <p>No issue, comment or status of the job holds:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>a <code>report_id</code>, an event ID, a <code>week</code> or a <code>client</code>;</li>
    <li>any count: no number of reports, no <code>count</code> bucket, no <code>retries</code>;</li>
    <li>any day;</li>
    <li><code>bootc_version</code>, <code>shipped</code>, or <code>secure</code> outside the 2 kinds above;</li>
    <li>a host name, a user name, a path on your computer, or free text.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="public-bug-reports-residue">What an issue still tells</h3>
  <p>An issue tells that 1 or more computers with reporting turned on sent a report with these values. Its creation time tells about when the first report came. A rare image and version can point to the few computers that run it.</p>

  <h3 id="public-bug-reports-cutoff">Which faults are filed</h3>
  <p>The job files only faults that our GlitchTip first saw on or after <time datetime={cutoffIso}>{cutoffText}</time>, 00:00 UTC. A fault first seen before then is never filed. If you turned reporting on before this section was published, you are not asked again: this section is the notice. You can turn reporting off at any time.</p>

  <h3 id="public-bug-reports-deletion">Deletion and turning reporting off</h3>
  <p>Deleting a report, or turning reporting off, does not remove, change or close an issue. An issue holds no identifier of a report, and only values that exist in Frostyard's public source.</p>

  <h2 id="your-rights">Your rights</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Consent.</strong> Consent is the legal basis for reports (GDPR Article 6(1)(a)). You can withdraw it at any time: turn reporting off.</li>
    <li><strong>See what was sent.</strong> ChairLift's <em>Sent reports</em> list shows the 50 newest reports it sent, each with its <code>report_id</code>. By default, ChairLift keeps the <code>report_id</code> of up to 500 sent reports in <code>~/.local/state/chairlift/reports.json</code>. On Floe, the list is <code>/var/lib/snosi-health/sent.jsonl</code>. A send run that posts at least 1 report and does not stop early drops the entries older than 90 days. Any other run drops nothing. A run that starts after reporting is turned off drops nothing. Only a run already in progress can still finish its cleanup. After that, the file keeps its entries until an administrator deletes it. (<a href="https://github.com/frostyard/snosi/blob/main/docs/adr/0030-floe-sends-problem-reports-with-admin-consent.md">snosi ADR-0030</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Delete a report.</strong> Send us its <code>report_id</code> and we delete that report. GlitchTip stores each report under its <code>report_id</code>, so we can find it. Backup snapshots keep it at most 7 more days. A deleted report stays in the statistics data points and the weekly totals, which hold no <code>report_id</code>. A <a href="#public-bug-reports">public bug report</a> also stays. Without the <code>report_id</code> we cannot tell which report is yours, because reports hold no identifier (GDPR Article 11).</li>
    <li><strong>Complain.</strong> You can complain to a data protection supervisory authority.</li>
    <li><strong>No automated decisions.</strong> Reports are not used to make any decision about you.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="who-is-responsible">Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>Controller: {controller}</p>
  <p>Contact for questions and deletion requests: <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a></p>

  <h2 id="transfers">Transfers outside the EU</h2>
  <p>Reports are kept in the United States. Cloudflare processes requests on its global network, under its data processing agreement and the EU–US Data Privacy Framework.</p>

  <h2 id="source">Source</h2>
  <p>Every part of reporting is open source.</p>
  <ul>{sources.map(([label, href, text]) => <li><a href={href}>{label}</a>: {text}</li>)}</ul>
  <p>The history of this page is in <a href="https://github.com/frostyard/frostyard-org/commits/main/src/pages/privacy/problem-reports.astro">frostyard/frostyard-org</a>.</p>
</Document>
